  "worked_solution": "First, we take the variable subset gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}\\,f & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x \\\\ 2z\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the second derivatives with respect to $x$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)=2\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x)=0 \\\\  & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2z)=0\\,\\, &  & \\frac{∂}{∂z}(2z)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x,z}({∇}_{x,z}\\,f) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂z} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂z∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
